## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running, pass the token into it:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. CPU-bound work without checkpoints won't stop. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using` it — leaks timers otherwise. |
| **`Task.WaitAsync` doesn't kill the work** | It unblocks the *caller*, but the task may still run in the background. The work must check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` to actually stop. |
| **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`)** | Move it to a child `Process` and call `Process.Kill()` on timeout — this is the only true "abort" in modern .NET. |

### What was removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` (implicit via timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Not needed — cooperative model |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` |

### Checklist

- ✅ No `Thread.Abort` / `SYSLIB0006`
- ✅ No `ThreadAbortException` catch
- ✅ `CancellationTokenSource` disposed via `using`
- ✅ Timeout produces same `TimeoutException` for callers
- ⚠️ If `work` doesn't check the token, it continues running silently after timeout — design callers accordingly
